<h2>7. Birth preparation is more useful than a perfect birth prediction</h2>
<p>Know the route, the bag, your partner’s preferences, and the care team’s instructions about when to call. Understand that the plan can change without anyone having failed.</p>
<p>Read <a href="/blog/what-should-a-dad-do-during-labor">what a dad should do during labor</a>, then talk through how she wants you to support her. You are preparing to respond well, not trying to control the outcome.</p>
<h2 id="baby-care">8. The boring repetitions build confidence</h2>
<p>Your first diaper change might take a ridiculous amount of concentration. Keep doing it. Competence comes from repetitions, not from waiting until you feel naturally good at baby care.</p>
<p>When someone demonstrates a task, try it yourself with help available. If you always hand the baby back at the first sign of uncertainty, you miss the practice that would make the next attempt easier.</p>
<h2>9. Bonding may grow through ordinary care</h2>
<p>Some dads feel an immediate rush. Others feel protective, stunned, or mostly focused on what needs doing. Do not turn someone else’s first moment into a test of your own.</p>
<p>Hold your baby safely while awake, talk to them, change them, and learn their cues. Keep showing up for those small interactions. If disconnection or distress is persistent or troubling, talk with a qualified health professional.</p>
<h2>10. Feeding is a team responsibility</h2>
<p>If your partner is breastfeeding, you can handle the surrounding work: water, food, clean equipment where needed, diapers, and settling afterward. If you are using bottles, learn the preparation, cleaning, and storage instructions yourself.</p>
<p>Follow the feeding plan you develop with your baby’s care team. When something is confusing or not going well, ask for help rather than trying to win an argument about which method is best.</p>
<h2>11. Safe sleep is worth learning before the first exhausted night</h2>
<p>Place your baby on their back for sleep, on a firm, flat infant sleep surface with a fitted sheet. Keep pillows, loose blankets, and toys out. Use a separate infant sleep space rather than sharing an adult sleep surface. The <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/reproductive-health/features/babies-sleep.html">CDC safe sleep guidance</a> explains these basics.</p>
<p>If you are getting sleepy with the baby in your arms, put them down in their safe sleep space or hand them to an awake caregiver. Do not try to outlast exhaustion on the couch.</p>
<h2>12. Plan for the night you will have, not the one you want</h2>
<p>Work out who can cover which tasks around feeding and recovery. Review the plan when it stops working. A newborn’s needs will not always fit tidy shifts, but you can still make the workload more deliberate.</p>
<p>A sleep resource can help you learn what questions to ask and what is appropriate for your baby’s stage. It should not replace the feeding or medical advice specific to your child.</p>
