<h2 id="relationship">13. Recovery deserves as much planning as the nursery</h2>
<p>Your partner is recovering while everyone is excited about the baby. Ask what she needs. Keep her discharge instructions available, help with practical tasks, and contact her care team with concerns.</p>
<p>Food, laundry, transport, and a boundary with visitors may do more for her day than another baby outfit. Put those jobs on your own list.</p>
<h2>14. Owning a task is different from waiting to be asked</h2>
<p>If you handle laundry, notice when it needs doing and finish the cycle all the way to putting it away. If you handle groceries, track what is running low. The noticing is part of the task.</p>
<p>Agree on responsibilities while you are both relatively rested. “Tell me what to do” is occasionally useful in a busy moment. It is exhausting as the household operating system.</p>
<h2>15. Keep score of what needs doing, not who has suffered more</h2>
<p>There will be nights when you both feel you have given everything. Turning that into a contest usually leaves two tired people defending themselves.</p>
<p>Try a concrete request: “I am struggling to stay awake. Can we work out the next hour?” You can name your limits and still recognize hers. Revisit bigger fairness questions when you have enough bandwidth to hear each other.</p>
<h2>16. Protect time together without making it another obligation</h2>
<p>A relationship check in can be ten quiet minutes asking how each other is doing. It does not need a reservation. Ask about the person, not just the next feeding or supply run.</p>
<p>Affection and intimacy may change during pregnancy and recovery. Listen, respect comfort and consent, and follow clinical guidance where relevant. Keep kindness available without making it a transaction.</p>
<BlogPhoto src="/images/John+and+Viv-40.webp" alt="John and Vivian smiling at each other while holding their newborn twins at home" ratio="2/3" />
<h2>17. Visitors should make life easier</h2>
<p>Decide together who you want around, for how long, and what would help. You can welcome someone and still say that today is a short visit.</p>
<p>Give willing helpers a specific job: bring dinner, take the dog out, or pick up groceries. Handle the communication with your own relatives so your recovering partner is not responsible for everyone’s expectations.</p>
<h2 id="support">18. Have another dad you can tell the truth to</h2>
<p>You need somewhere to ask the question you think you should already know the answer to. A friend, a relative, or a dad community can give you perspective and a place to talk.</p>
<p>That is part of why Dudela exists. Bring the awkward question, the practical question, or the one you have been carrying around for a week.</p>
<BlogOffer heading="Bring the question you are actually worried about" body="Spit Up Society gives you a place to ask John and Mike your real dad questions, with live Q&A twice a month and a private community." href="/join/spit-up-society" label="Join Spit Up Society" />

<h2>19. Persistent distress deserves more than pushing through</h2>
<p>Some days will be rough. If worry, low mood, anger, or disconnection persists or interferes with daily life, speak with a qualified health professional. You do not need to reach a breaking point before asking for help.</p>
<p>Our article on <a href="/blog/emotional-side-of-fatherhood">the emotional side of fatherhood</a> can help start a conversation. If you or anyone else is in immediate danger, contact emergency services.</p>
<h2>20. Repair matters more than pretending you never get it wrong</h2>
<p>You will miss a cue, snap when you are tired, or forget something important. Name what you did, apologize without attaching a defense, and change the part you can change.</p>
<p>“I was short with you. That was unfair. I need a minute, then I will handle the dishes” is more useful than a long explanation of why you are the tired one.</p>
<h2>21. Decide what kind of dad you want to practice being</h2>
<p>Choose a few qualities you want your family to experience: patient, dependable, playful, honest. Then make one visible in the day you already have. Put the phone away during a feed. Follow through on the errand. Admit when you do not know.</p>
<p>You do not become that dad by writing the perfect statement about fatherhood. You practice him in ordinary moments, and you start again when you miss one.</p>
<h2 id="this-week">Your first week of preparation</h2>
<p>Pick three moves: one conversation with your partner, one practical task you will fully own, and one thing you will learn. That might be talking about visitors, confirming leave, and learning safe sleep basics. Next week, choose the next three.</p>
